1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,507
The brush uncovers a manuscript, and it is not a hymn, a story or a prayer.

2
00:00:05,507 --> 00:00:11,929
It is a manual: how to raise revenue, how to run a bureaucracy, how to handle spies, famine and war.

3
00:00:13,267 --> 00:00:19,804
Somebody in ancient India sat down and wrote the most unsentimental book about power the subcontinent produced.

4
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:31,473
From the Arthashastra itself.

5
00:00:31,473 --> 00:00:35,810
From Greek accounts, especially Megasthenes, who came as an ambassador.

6
00:00:35,810 --> 00:00:40,507
From Jain and Buddhist traditions, which preserve stories about Chandragupta.

7
00:00:40,507 --> 00:00:42,254
And from a few inscriptions.

8
00:00:42,254 --> 00:00:46,589
These sources disagree with each other more than you might expect.

9
00:00:50,667 --> 00:00:55,423
Around 321 BCE, Chandragupta Maurya overthrew the Nandas and took Magadha.

10
00:00:55,423 --> 00:01:00,687
The traditions say he was helped by Kautilya, also called Chanakya or Vishnugupta,

11
00:01:00,687 --> 00:01:04,475
a Brahmin teacher from Taxila who became his minister.

12
00:01:08,133 --> 00:01:14,556
How much of that partnership is history and how much is later storytelling is debated — but the empire is not in doubt.

13
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:18,781
He expanded fast.

14
00:01:18,781 --> 00:01:26,098
Magadha first, then north-west into the territory Alexander's successors held, then south into the Deccan.

15
00:01:28,267 --> 00:01:32,340
Within a generation the Mauryas controlled more of the subcontinent than any Indi

16
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:36,762
an state had before — the first time so much of it answered to one administration.

17
00:01:40,267 --> 00:01:45,998
Around 305 BCE he met Seleucus Nicator, who ruled the eastern part of Alexander's empire.

18
00:01:45,998 --> 00:01:54,640
The sources record a settlement: Seleucus ceded territory in the north-west, and Chandragupta gave him five hundred war elephants.

19
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:02,482
A marriage alliance is mentioned too, though the details are unclear.

20
00:02:02,482 --> 00:02:05,869
Seleucus then sent Megasthenes as his ambassador to Pataliputra,

21
00:02:05,869 --> 00:02:09,922
which is why we have a Greek description of the Mauryan capital at all.

22
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,009
And then the Arthashastra, attributed to Kautilya, which is where the theory lives.

23
00:02:19,009 --> 00:02:23,660
Its most quoted idea is the saptanga, the seven limbs of the state: the king,

24
00:02:23,660 --> 00:02:29,268
the ministers, the territory and people, the fort, the treasury, the army, and the ally.

25
00:02:32,067 --> 00:02:35,277
A state, it argues, is healthy only when all seven are.

26
00:02:35,277 --> 00:02:38,888
Its other famous idea is the mandala theory of foreign policy:

27
00:02:38,888 --> 00:02:44,404
your neighbour is your likely enemy, and your neighbour's neighbour is your likely friend.

28
00:02:47,967 --> 00:02:49,366
A caution about the book.

29
00:02:49,366 --> 00:02:56,929
Some scholars treat it as substantially Kautilya's, written in the Mauryan period, and read Mauryan administration directly out of it.

30
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:16,938
Others argue it reached its present form several centuries later, as a compilation,

31
00:03:16,938 --> 00:03:23,254
and that using it to describe Chandragupta's government is reading a later textbook back onto an earlier state.

32
00:03:23,254 --> 00:03:27,202
Most now take a middle position: an older core, with later additions.

33
00:03:27,202 --> 00:03:32,014
Treat it as political theory first, and as evidence of practice only with care.

34
00:03:33,333 --> 00:03:36,656
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

35
00:03:36,656 --> 00:03:41,193
King, ministers, territory, fort, treasury, army, ally.

36
00:03:41,193 --> 00:03:43,600
Seven limbs of the state.

37
00:03:47,633 --> 00:03:48,385
Dig Quiz.

38
00:03:48,385 --> 00:03:49,814
Pause and name it.

39
00:03:49,814 --> 00:03:55,377
First: the Arthashastra's model of the state as seven essential elements.

40
00:03:55,377 --> 00:03:56,730
The saptanga.

41
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:05,072
Second: its theory that a neighbour is a likely enemy and the neighbour's neighbour a likely friend.

42
00:04:05,072 --> 00:04:06,495
The mandala theory.

43
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:16,641
Third: the Greek ambassador sent to Pataliputra, whose account survives only in fragments quoted by others.

44
00:04:16,641 --> 00:04:17,837
Megasthenes.

45
00:04:19,167 --> 00:04:25,501
In Mains, a good question asks how far the Arthashastra can be used as evidence for Mauryan administration.

46
00:04:25,501 --> 00:04:29,672
Answer it as a question about dating and genre, not just content.

47
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:41,189
A manual of statecraft takes its place in the gallery: the most clear-eyed book about power that ancient India left us.

48
00:04:44,167 --> 00:04:51,466
Next gallery: his grandson fights one war, and then spends the rest of his reign explaining why he will not fight another.

49
00:04:51,466 --> 00:04:55,148
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

